    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q2-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The source first fits the proposed form using three successive terms: From u1 = 1/5, the recurrence gives u2 = 1/(8/5 + 1) = 5/13, u3 = (25/13)/(40/13 + 1) = 25/53. Therefore k + c = 1 1 5 k + c = 5 c = 5 −k ka + c = 5 a 13 ka + c = 13a 5 ka + 5 −k = 13 5 a k(a −1) = 13 5 a −5 a2 ka2 + c = 25 53 ka2 + c = 53 25a2 ka2 + 5 −k = 53 25a2 k(a2 −1) = 53 25a2 −5\" style=\"--madas-width:536.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q2-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Eliminate k by multiplying the first relation by a + 1 and comparing the second: Clearing 25 gives (a + 1)(65a −125) = 53a2 −125 65a2 −125a + 65a −125 = 53a2 −125 12a2 −60a = 0 12a(a −5) = 0 a = 5 a̸ = 0 k(a −1) = 13 5 a −5 4k = 13 −5 Thus k = 2, c = 5 −k = 3. The recurrence itself verifies that this form holds for every n. The terms are positive by induction, so set vn = 1/un. [M1] [A1] Then\" style=\"--madas-width:518.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q2-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"vn+1 = 1 5vn + 8 5, v1 = 5. [M1] [M1] [A1] Subtracting the fixed point 2 gives vn+1 −2 = (vn −2)/5, [M1] [A1] so vn = 2 + 3(1/5)n−1. [M1] [A1] Taking reciprocals, 5n−1 un = 3 + 2 · 5n−1 , a = 5, c = 3, k = 2. [M1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 2 is 12 marks.</p>"
    ],
