    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A curve C has equation x− x ,  x∈ , y = x &gt; 0 . Show that y is a solution of the equation 2 2 2   d y dy y y =   − . ( ) 8 2  dx  x dx\" style=\"--madas-width:589.5px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Take logarithms of y = x−x to get ln y = −x ln x. [M1] The logarithm changes x−x into a product with two x-dependent factors: Differentiate the left side as (1/y)(dy/dx). Implicit differentiation and the product rule give y′/y = −ln x −x(1/x) . [M1] = −ln x −1 Thus y′ = −y(1 + ln x). [A1] Differentiate this form again, using the product rule: y′′ = −y′(1 + ln x) −y/x. [M1] [A1] Here d(1 + ln x)/dx = 1/x, giving the term −y/x. Rearrange the first derivative as 1 + ln x = −y′/y. [M1] Substitution gives y′′ = −y′(−y′/y) −y/x . [M1] = (y′)2/y −y/x\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q3-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Multiply by y to obtain yy′′ = (y′)2 −y2/x . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 8 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A curve C has equation x− x ,  x∈ , y = x > 0 . Show that y is a solution of the equation 2 2 2   d y dy y y =   − . ( ) 8 2  dx  x dx"
  },
