    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) P = (1/2, −1/2). [B1] Since dx/dt = −sin t and dy/dt = −2 sin 2t, [A1] (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = 2 sin 2t/ sin t . = 4 cos t Thus the tangent gradient at t = π/3 is 4 cos(π/3) = 2. [A1] The normal has slope −1/2, giving y + 1/2 = −1 2(x −1/2) [A1] which expands to 4y + 2 = −2x + 1, or 2x + 4y + 1 = 0 . [A1] (b) The curve has y = 2x2 −1 [B1] [A1] for −1 ≤x ≤1. [M1] [A1] The source substitutes x = cos t, y = cos 2t in the line to find the second point: 2 cos t + 4 cos 2t + 1 = 0 2 cos t + 4(2 cos2 t −1) + 1 = 0 2 cos t + 8 cos2 t −3 = 0\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"8 cos2 t + 2 cos t −3 = 0 (4 cos t + 3)(2 cos t −1) = 0 The first factor gives cos t = −3/4, the second the original point. Substitute into the normal to obtain 8x2 + 2x −3 = (2x −1)(4x + 3) . [A1] = 0 The other root is −3/4 and lies in the domain, giving Q = (−3/4, 1/8) . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 12 marks.</p>"
