    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-y/q11",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q11-question-1.svg\" alt=\"cm s−. 3 1 Liquid is pouring into a container at the constant rate of 12π The container is initially empty and when the height of the liquid in the container is 3 h cm the volume of the liquid, V cm , is given by V = π h h ( + 20 ) . Determine the rate at which the height of the liquid in the container is rising 8 seconds after the liquid started pouring in. ( 10 )\" style=\"--madas-width:584.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The inflow gives dV/dt = 12π. Since V = πh(h + 20) = π(h2 + 20h), we have dV/dh = 2π(h + 10). [B1] [A1] Use the chain rule in the form dh/dt = (dh/dV )(dV/dt). [M1] Substituting the reciprocal derivative gives dh/dt = 12π/[2π(h + 10)] . [M1] [A1] = 6/(h + 10)\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-y/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"After eight seconds, V = 8(12π) . [A1] = 96π Thus 96 = h2 + 20h, [A1] giving (h −4)(h + 24) = 0. [A1] The two algebraic roots are 4, −24; a negative depth is physically impossible: Only h = 4 is a possible depth: [A1] At this instant, dh/dt = 6/(4 + 10) = 3/7 cm s−1 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 11 is 10 marks.</p>"
